  "live_records": {
    "q-40586": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40586",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x=e$ above the $x$-axis. What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272242/assets/179c98fca14b2cc3e14a561b1217cd4b583cfc41f21170fbe7571fcb8d0fb371.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272242/assets/627010a2f845b4f3cf3fb5f63d0701a611053b6ec49a976ea0b6b1013d1f2b10.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{e}^{y}\\le x\\le e\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{e}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
