    "q-132561": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132561",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272242/assets/4de0680e2b6f29892a44fb114653e01433519ae01401d528a1e0263d6577003b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}3y\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ and $y=2x,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}} \\\\ y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ 5-{y}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ 20-4{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272242/assets/db2d8b0a039490c1065fdb55771f47d36da4b6480e398b8bf8aea952fc2e8012.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}3y\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}}3y\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}}3y\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y[{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y[x]_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3y(\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}y)dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3y\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3y\\sqrt{5-{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2})dy & ={[-(5-{y}^{2})^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-4)-(-5\\sqrt{5}-0)) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{5}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132561-a",
